From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 16:07:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: serve the shell over domicile:// and bind the
 control channel

The shell was served over a loopback HTTP port whose WebSocket was a byte pipe
to the compositor's control socket -- and that protocol carries Spawn, which is
arbitrary commands on the machine running the desktop. A loopback port is
reachable by every process on the box and no header fixes that. What fixes it
is not having a port.

Two halves, and this is the engine's. domicile:// serves the shell so the page
has a real origin without a port behind it, and the control channel becomes an
interface the browser binds for the shell's origin rather than a socket the
page opens.

FIVE INDEPENDENT GATES stand between a registered scheme and a page that
loads, and each was found by running it rather than by reading:

  1. ContentClient::AddAdditionalSchemes           -- gives it an origin
  2. ProfileIOData::IsHandledURL                   -- or the URL silently
                                                      becomes the New Tab page
  3. ValidateLaunchUrlWebUnsafe                    -- or it cannot be launched
                                                      from the command line
  4. MaybeLaunchAppShortcutWindow                  -- or --app= is dropped and
                                                      the window is an ordinary
                                                      one on the New Tab page
  5. ChildProcessSecurityPolicy                    -- or the document commits
                                                      and every subresource in
                                                      it fails

Gates 3 and 4 are where the design's own choice bites: launching by URL
requires a web-safe scheme, and domicile:// is deliberately not web-safe. Those
two lists answer different questions -- web-safe is "may a web page navigate
here", this is "may the command line ask for this" -- and the command line is
trusted. chrome:// sits in the same list for the same reason.

They are two checks, not one, and that is the trap: url_util.cc guards a URL
given as a bare argument, startup_browser_creator.cc guards the same URL given
to --app=, and patching the first leaves the second. Gate 4 also fails in the
quietest way of the five. It does not refuse; it declines, and startup carries
on as if --app= had never been passed -- so a desktop launches as a browser
window on about:newtab, no line is logged on either side, and the compositor
sits waiting for a chrome that will never speak to it.

Gate 4 is granted per process, never with RegisterWebSafeIsolatedScheme, which
would let any renderer request domicile:// URLs. GrantRequestScheme only, never
GrantCommitScheme: committing is what confers the origin, and the origin is
what the control channel keys on.

THE ACCESS CONTROL IS ONE CONDITION, and it is checked at bind time rather than
when the binder map is built. The map is per-RenderFrameHost and is populated
before any document commits, so gating there reads an empty origin, registers
nothing, and Chromium then kills the renderer for requesting an unbound
interface. That is a mistake that looks like it is working.

The channel runs on the IO thread because net::SocketPosix DCHECKs it, and the
hop is done in chrome/ so components/domicile keeps its property of not
depending on //content.

Verified end to end against a stand-in compositor. The page:
  {"origin":"domicile://shell","hostCtor":"DomicileHost","probeRan":true,
   "titled":{"appId":"term","title":"kitty"},"spawnSent":true}
The compositor:
  RECV {"protocol_version": 1, "type": "hello"}
  RECV {"command": ["kitty"], "type": "spawn"}

So navigation, subresources, the origin, the binding, an outbound message and
an unsolicited inbound one all work. What does not yet exist is the other 27
protocol members, and shellDocument still lives in TypeScript.
